<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/thermal-low-end-device-strategy.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">The demo is smooth for a minute, then video previews compete with scrolling as the device warms. A fixed “high quality” setting cannot express the smaller work budget available later in the session.</p>

Make expensive features respond to a shared, reversible policy and verify long-session behavior on the devices the product supports.

<!-- @after Graceful Degradation Matrix :: visual -->

### Reduce optional work, preserve the task

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Observe pressure</strong><small>Thermal/device signals and measured journey behavior.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Choose capability policy</strong><small>Reduce speculative work or quality where the product allows it.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Protect continuity</strong><small>Keep interaction and required state safe; recover quality deliberately.</small></div></div><figcaption>Policy examples, not platform-prescribed thresholds. Thermal status reports thermal pressure; it does not promise an exact CPU frequency or safe workload.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Thermal-Aware Work Scheduling :: animation -->

### A long session changes the available budget

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A long session changes the available budget</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="normal" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Normal policy</strong><small>Full approved experience</small></div><div class="node" data-el="pressure" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Pressure observed</strong><small>Thermal signal or degraded evidence</small></div><div class="node" data-el="reduced" data-art="gate" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Reduced policy</strong><small>Pause optional prefetch</small></div><div class="node" data-el="recover" data-art="clock" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Recovery policy</strong><small>Restore after stable conditions</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Begin", "caption": "Run the real journey long enough to exercise sustained load. The animation is illustrative and contains no measured device temperatures.", "state": {"normal": "active"}, "add": ["normal", "pressure", "reduced", "recover"]}, {"label": "Observe", "caption": "Pressure changes while the session continues. A shared capability layer receives the signal rather than each feature inventing its own rule.", "state": {"normal": "warn", "pressure": "active"}}, {"label": "Degrade", "caption": "Pause optional prefetch or reduce visual quality within approved product limits. Required state and critical operations keep their correctness guarantees.", "state": {"pressure": "done", "reduced": "active"}}, {"label": "Recover", "caption": "Restore optional work under a deliberate policy. A stability interval or hysteresis can prevent rapid toggling; that is an app policy, not an Android guarantee.", "state": {"reduced": "done", "recover": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Observability :: staff -->

Define which optional work may degrade on the affected device cohort and what minimum product behavior remains available. For a long media or camera session, verify recovery as well as the pressure response: a policy that permanently leaves quality low after conditions improve creates another defect. Keep session duration, device tier and thermal conditions with the result. Decide thresholds from those observations and product needs, not from a fleet average that hides struggling users.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Does moving heavy CPU work off Main remove thermal cost?</summary>

No. It can protect interaction while the CPU still performs the same work.

</details>

<details><summary>Why test longer than the happy-path demo?</summary>

Heat, cache growth and sustained contention may emerge only during extended use.

</details>

<details><summary>Who decides acceptable degraded quality?</summary>

The product and feature owners, using measured device behavior and correctness requirements.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Thermal status API](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/os/PowerManager), [Device performance adaptation](https://developer.android.com/games/optimize/adpf/thermal).

